WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:05.940
Joshua Oppenheimer: There was this weird opportunity
to do something unprecedented in the history

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:11.370
of nonfiction film. To confront a perpetrator
while the perpetrator still holds a monopoly

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.690
on power.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:18.779
Joe Posner (Vox): This is Joshua Oppenheimer

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:24.439
His two films are the most frightening, and
moving I have ever seen. They're essentially

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:37.230
a series of confrontations with the perpetrators
of a horrifying chapter in Indonesia’s history:

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:44.010
Oppenheimer: In 1965 there was a right wing
military coup. The military took over Indonesia

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.980
with the help of the united states and other
western countries. And within 6 months to

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:54.730
a year, somewhere between half a million and
two and a half million people were killed.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:01:00.179
Anyone who was seen as unlikely to just embrace
the new dictatorship was accused of being

00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:01.489
a communist and killed.

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:05.940
Posner: First, In The Act of Killing, he confronts
people who carried out these killings with,

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:13.080
essentially, a mirror.Never held to account,
they boast. So Oppenheimer asks them to dramatize

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:13.720
their actions.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.410
Oppenheimer: I said. You’ve participated
in one of the biggest killings in human history.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:23.140
I want to know what it means to you and to
your society. You want to show me what you’ve

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.640
done. So show me what you’ve done in whatever
way you wish, I will film your dramatizations

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.170
– and they’re not re-enactments, they’re
dramatizations. Because re-enactments are

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:36.020
about what happened in the past, dramatizations
are actually acting out how they want to be

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.160
seen in the present.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:41.240
Posner: The perpetrators involved start to
realize that they will not be seen as heroes.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.680
Oppenheimer knew releasing the film would
mean likely never going back to Indonesia,

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:57.700
but it created an opportunity.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.910
Oppenheimer: I was perceived to be close to
the highest ranking perpetrators in the country.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:06.890
Posner: Before release, he orchestrated a
second set of even more direct confrontations.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:17.270
Oppenheimer: Adi the youngest son, in a family
of survivors, his oldest brother was killed,

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.380
he feels trapped in this kind of prison of
fear as a survivor that comes from living

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:26.360
surrounded by the still powerful perpetrators.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:40.720
He feels the need to go to them, in the hope
that he will be able to make peace with his

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:41.420
neighbors.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:01.030
We had to take many safety precautions. While
shooting including bringing a second car,

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:07.840
having Adi’s at the airport ready to evacuate.
Adi would come with no id, no telephone, so

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.960
they wouldn’t be able to figure out
who he was until we were able to get help.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.920
The 
Indonesian government perhaps in response
to the changes these two films have helped

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:34.240
to catalyze, has introduced a truth and reconciliation
bill in the parliament. It’ll be a struggle,

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.740
though, to make sure that bill has teeth and
is credible.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:44.300
The oligarchs in Indonesia,the wealthiest,
the most powerful, will not want their power

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:48.970
and wealth exposed as the fruits of plunder
and atrocity.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.080
Posner: But there’s another perpetrator
confronted in these films, too.

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:55.660
Oppenheimer: There’s a moment in the Look
of Silence where a perpetrator looks straight

00:03:55.660 --> 00:04:00.680
at me because he knows i’m an American and
says “we killed because we know the Americans

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:05.120
told us to kill the communists. The Americans
taught us to hate the communists.” And then

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.400
there’s a long pause while he looks at the
camera – and of course he’s looking at me.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:14.300
But because, just as in this interview i’m
looking at the camera and at the audience,

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:18.820
he’s also looking at the audience. And for
an American audience, it’s a direct implication.

00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:25.840
The details that have come to the surface
are damning. To have an embassy official who’s

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:31.319
compiling a list of thousands of names asking
that they be killed.

00:04:31.319 --> 00:04:35.999
It’s directly implicating all of us. The
film is a mirror in which we hopefully see ourselves.

